  "routeSelection": {
    "route": "Strategy and Organization specialization",
    "selectionRule": "broad-academic-identity",
    "basis": "The normalized target is the generic International Business Administration bachelor, and the adopted 2026-2027 programme schedule requires one of eleven year-3 specializations, each fixing two 6-EC courses and the subject of the 12-EC thesis, so one must be instantiated. The regulations define the programme as international business administration and state that it prepares students for a subsequent master's in Business Administration or Entrepreneurship; its compulsory core includes Organization Theory, International Strategy and Business Processes. Accounting, Economics, Finance, Financial Management, Marketing, Strategic Human Resource Management / Leadership and Change Management, Digital Business Innovation and Transport and Supply Chain Management each deepen one functional discipline, while Entrepreneurship and Management Consulting apply strategy to a particular setting. Strategy and Organization is the general-management specialization of business administration: both of its courses list the compulsory Organization Theory and International Strategy courses as recommended background, and Foundations of Strategic Management is also the shared first course of the Entrepreneurship and Management Consulting specializations. It therefore best represents the programme's broad business-administration identity. The choice was made from the official evidence alone and does not improve the UCR match: both specialization rows and the thesis remain without a UCR counterpart."
  },
  "identity": "English-language full-time International Business Administration, 2026–2027. Adopted SBE teaching regulations and annual chart, exact course records and exchange rules establish the selected 180 EC example.",
  "progression": "The adopted 2026–2027 schedule has 60 EC in each year. Year one has ten compulsory courses. Year two has eight compulsory courses and two 6 EC electives, one in each of periods 4 and 5. The selected electives are Accounting, Business and Society and Corporate Finance: A Sustainability Perspective. Year three contains a 30 EC exchange, 6 EC Ethics, two 6 EC Strategy and Organization courses, and the 12 EC individual thesis. The adopted OER ties the English diagnostic test or refresher to Academic Skills. A public Study Guide description instead names Global Sustainability for this cohort. This example follows the adopted rule and completes the requirement before either grade. Research Participation I and II are zero-credit requirements for receiving Business Processes and Integrative Research Project credits, respectively. Quantitative Business Research Methods requires Business Mathematics. Exchange normally requires 60 EC at application, a completed first year and 90 EC before departure, and approval of the planned courses. The illustrated route completes years one and two before exchange. The thesis requires Academic Skills and both Business Research Methods courses, registration and supervisor selection. Students undertake supervised individual research in the chosen specialisation.",
